.TH "NETQUALITY" "1" "" "netquality 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netquality \- tray indicator grading network quality for interactive use
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]netquality\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-target\f[R] \f[I]HOST\f[R]]
[\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]netquality\f[R] puts coloured signal bars in the system tray.
It grades the connection as good, fair, poor, bad or down.
.PP
\f[B]netquality\f[R] does not measure throughput.
It measures the conditions that make an interactive session usable.
It puts each probe in one of three classes: OK, LATE or LOST.
The class depends on a deadline (\f[B]\-\-deadline\f[R]).
A reply that comes too late to use is an impairment.
It is not a success.
.PP
\f[B]netquality\f[R] also counts the longest run of bad probes in
sequence.
A frozen session and scattered packet loss can have the same loss rate.
The run length tells them apart.
.PP
The worst impairment sets the grade.
The menu gives the name of the rule that set the colour.
Thus the colour is always clear.
.PP
The correct deadline depends on the traffic.
Therefore \f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] sets the deadline, the limits and the
menu words together.
Use \f[I]ssh\f[R] for typed commands, \f[I]realtime\f[R] for voice and
video, and \f[I]web\f[R] for a browser.
A limit that you give on the command line replaces the limit from the
profile.
There is no profile for throughput, because ICMP round\-trip time cannot
measure bandwidth.
.PP
The probe rate changes with the conditions.
The rate is slow when the link is good.
The rate increases when the link becomes unstable.
A link that is slow but steady does not increase the rate, because more
probes give no new data.
Thus you can use \f[B]netquality\f[R] on a metered link.
The idle cost is approximately 1.9 MB each day.
.PP
You can give \f[B]\-\-target\f[R] more than one time.
The first target sets the icon and the grade.
\f[B]netquality\f[R] probes the other targets only during an alert, thus
more targets do not increase the cost when the link is good.
When the link is good, the menu shows each other target as
\(dqstandby\(dq.
More than one target shows if a fault is one destination or all traffic.
.PP
\f[B]netquality\f[R] also probes the first hop to each target, and
judges that hop against stricter local limits.
This shows if a fault is on your own link or after it.
\f[B]netquality\f[R] asks the kernel which hop it uses for each target.
It does not use the default route, which can be a different link on a
machine with more than one uplink.
\f[B]netquality\f[R] probes each address one time, thus a hop that more
than one target uses is not probed two times.
.PP
You do not configure a tunnel.
A tunnel is only a first hop that goes out through a tunnel device.
\f[B]netquality\f[R] does not apply local\-link limits to such a hop,
and never reports it as down, because many tunnel endpoints discard ICMP
but carry traffic correctly.
To find how much delay the path to a VPN adds, and how much the tunnel
adds, give the VPN server as a second \f[B]\-\-target\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]netquality\f[R] needs an AppIndicator host.
On MATE, add the Indicator Applet Complete to the panel.
The Notification Area applet does not show \f[B]netquality\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
show this help message and exit
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
show program\(aqs version number and exit
.TP
\f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] \f[I]PROFILE\f[R]
the use of the link: ssh for typed commands, realtime for voice and
video, web for a browser.
This sets the limits below and the menu words.
The defaults that follow are the ssh ones.
Default: \f[CR]ssh\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-target\f[R] \f[I]HOST\f[R]
a host to watch.
You can give this option more than one time.
The first target sets the icon and the grade.
The other targets are probed only during an alert, to show if a fault is
one destination or all traffic.
Default: 8.8.8.8
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-hops\f[R]
do not probe the first hop to each target
.TP
\f[B]\-\-hop\-recheck\f[R] \f[I]HOP_RECHECK\f[R]
the time between two checks of the first hop to each target, in seconds.
Default: \f[CR]10.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-hop\-divisor\f[R] \f[I]HOP_DIVISOR\f[R]
probe each first hop at this multiple of its target interval.
Default: \f[CR]4.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-hop\-bad\-ms\f[R] \f[I]HOP_BAD_MS\f[R]
the median RTT to a first hop that makes the local link suspect, in ms.
Default: \f[CR]30.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-payload\f[R] \f[I]PAYLOAD\f[R]
the size of the ICMP payload, in bytes.
Default: \f[CR]16\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-calm\-interval\f[R] \f[I]CALM_INTERVAL\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]5.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-busy\-interval\f[R] \f[I]BUSY_INTERVAL\f[R]
See \-\-help.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-alert\-hold\f[R] \f[I]ALERT_HOLD\f[R]
the time to stay in fast mode after the last bad probe, in seconds.
Default: \f[CR]60.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-wait\f[R] \f[I]WAIT\f[R]
the time to wait for one reply (ping \-W), in seconds.
Default: \f[CR]3.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-window\f[R] \f[I]WINDOW\f[R]
the length of the measurement window, in seconds.
Default: \f[CR]120.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-deadline\f[R] \f[I]DEADLINE\f[R]
an RTT more than this is LATE: too slow to use, in ms.
Default: \f[CR]1000.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-run\-poor\f[R] \f[I]RUN_POOR\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]2\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-run\-bad\f[R] \f[I]RUN_BAD\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]4\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-bad\-pct\-poor\f[R] \f[I]BAD_PCT_POOR\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]8.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-bad\-pct\-bad\f[R] \f[I]BAD_PCT_BAD\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]20.0\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-\-median\-fair\f[R] \f[I]MEDIAN_FAIR\f[R]
Default: \f[CR]120.0\f[R]
.TP
